(Title) Tj
T*
(A Crossover Study of Culinary-Range Turmeric and Pepper Extract Effects on Pain Perception) Tj
T*
(in Midlife and Older Adults with Persistent Pain) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Abstract) Tj
T*
(Chronic discomfort remains a significant public health challenge, with many individuals) Tj
T*
(seeking nutritional adjuncts alongside conventional therapies. This randomised crossover) Tj
T*
(trial will investigate whether commonly-available spice components at levels feasible) Tj
T*
(within typical dietary intake can influence pain perception. Adults aged 40 and above with) Tj
T*
(moderate baseline discomfort will be randomised to receive different formulations in a) Tj
T*
(crossover sequence. Each participant will report daily pain ratings via text-based prompts) Tj
T*
(using a standardised 0-10 scale, with assessments captured three times daily throughout) Tj
T*
(the intervention periods. The study will examine whether certain spice combinations) Tj
T*
(produce detectable changes in reported discomfort relative to placebo or single-ingredient) Tj
T*
(controls. Participants will also log any additional pain relief medications taken.) Tj
T*
(Analysis will employ mixed-effects regression to account for repeated measurements and) Tj
T*
(individual variation. This design aims to determine whether accessible, food-level) Tj
T*
(interventions warrant further development for pain management.) Tj
